course: dictionary
lesson: 3682
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word flaky is a good example. «Leave a bike out in the rain all winter and its chain and bolts turn orange-brown and flaky.» Here flaky means breaking off in thin, flat bits, because the metal is rusting.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. «Her skin was dry and flaky after the cold winter.» Here flaky describes skin that comes off in small bits. Ask yourself what the word is describing, and that will show you which meaning fits.
explain: Informal speech adds more meanings. «Ben is a bit flaky, so never count on him to turn up.» Flaky here has nothing to do with breaking. A flaky person is unreliable: they forget things or cancel plans. «The school wifi is flaky today» means it works only some of the time.
fact: Flaky means breaking off easily into thin, flat pieces called flakes.
fact: «Flaky pastry» is light and has many thin layers, like a croissant or a sausage roll.
fact: Flaky skin is dry and comes off in small bits, and cream can help it.
fact: In informal talk, a flaky friend is unreliable, someone you cannot count on.
fact: Something flaky, like a wifi connection or a computer program, works only some of the time.
exercise: «Rust is flaky and falls away, so fresh iron underneath is uncovered.» What does flaky mean here? | Very tasty | Cold and icy | *Breaking off easily into thin, flat pieces | Shiny and new
exercise: «The pastry was light and flaky, with thin layers that melted on the tongue.» What is flaky describing? | A friend who forgets plans | A cold, wet road | *Pastry with many thin layers | A slow computer
exercise: Jo says, «The school app is flaky, it logs me out every few minutes.» What does flaky mean? | *Works only some of the time | Very kind | Breaks into flakes like pastry | Very quiet
test: «Dry, flaky skin can feel tight, so put some cream on it.» What does flaky describe? | A shiny coat of paint | *Skin that is dry and comes off in small bits | A happy friend | Thin, flat pieces of bread
test: Ella says, «Dan is so flaky, he never remembers to bring the ball.» What does flaky tell us about Dan? | He is very tall | He is very hungry | He is made of pastry | *He is unreliable and cannot be counted on
exam: Which sentence uses flaky in the informal way, to mean unreliable? | *«Max is flaky: he cancels every plan at the last minute.» | «The rust is flaky and falls off the gate.» | «Dry, flaky skin needs cream.» | «The cake had flaky layers of pastry.»
